---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="commentary">
	<h1>commentary</h1>
		<article>
			<h2>Our CSS Isn't Opinionated Enough</h2>
			<p>
				Just read an article about how &nbsp;
				<a href="https://www.craigabbott.co.uk/blog/2026/our-css-isnt-opinionated-enough/">
					CSS isn't opinionated enough
				</a>.
				As part of that, Craig argues that we should do more styling with element tags and
				using accessibility attribute selectors (e.g. <code>[aria-current="page"]</code>)
				to ensure that styles are only applied when the HTML semantics make sense for them
				to be. That article has a lot of good points, so definitely go and read it if you
				haven't seen it.
			</p>
			<p>
				On the whole, I'm not mad keen on Tailwind. There's a number of reasons for this,
				for example how it encourages you to write your styles horizontally, rather than
				having a clean left edge to easily scan. One of my biggest complaints is how it
				often leads to inconsistent styling of a given component/element type on a page.
				Granted, if you split things into components early and often, you can avoid this.
				But it's often very tempting to "just add a couple of classes here and there" and
				then... you've ended up with inconsistent styling.
			</p>
			<p>
				This article proposes that an over-reliance on classes can lead to using the wrong
				HTML element under the hood &mdash; something I'd never really considered before.
				In both cases however, writing CSS to target the correct elements and ARIA-lead
				exceptions encourages devs to do the right thing. Not by making it "more correct",
				but by making it easier. Reducing friction seems to be the most reliable way of
				getting people (myself included) to make better decisions.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you love Tailwind, this probably won't change your mind. You've probably got an
				article similar to this one which gives you similarly strong justification in the
				other direction. That's fine. But I'm always happy to have one more reason to advocate
				against using tech that doesn't work for my brain. (Just as you should for yours.)
			</p>
			<p>
				P.S. the stack I would use is pretty much CUBE CSS, albeit with a minimal utility
				layer. That being said, this is one place where I don't mind having Tailwind, as
				it does do that job very well. Although I'm now looking at using UnoCSS instead,
				as Tailwind V4's config system does expect you to be much more aligned with their
				default way of doing things. I'd also write a config file in standard JSON or JavaScript,
				using types or schemata that any editor can load and hint for, rather than relying on an
				extension to document a custom superset of CSS.
			</p>
		</article>

</BaseLayout>
